## Constructor parameters

```js
new WebSocket(url)
new WebSocket(url, protocols)
```

- **`url`** (required): The server URL. It must use `ws`, `wss`, `http`, or `https`, and it cannot contain a URL fragment. Relative URLs resolve against the calling script's base URL. `https` is converted to `wss`, so `WebSocket.url` reports the `wss:` form:

  ```js
  const httpsWebSocket = new WebSocket("https://websocket.example.org");
  console.log(httpsWebSocket.url); // 'wss://websocket.example.org'
  ```

- **`protocols`** (optional): A string or an array of strings naming the sub-protocols the client would like to use, in order of preference. Defaults to `[]`.

## Custom headers

The page does not document support for custom request headers. The constructor accepts only `url` and `protocols`, so the browser API offers no way to pass arbitrary headers. The only header-related input is the `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` value, which `protocols` controls.

## Subprotocol selection

- Values must be valid for the `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` header. They can be registered names from the IANA registry (such as `soap` or `wamp`) or custom names that the client and server both understand.
- Only one sub-protocol can be selected per connection.
- The connection is not established until the server negotiates a sub-protocol. The result is available in `WebSocket.protocol`, which is an empty string if the connection cannot be established.
- The constructor throws a `SyntaxError` if any value in `protocols` appears more than once or doesn't meet the header's syntax requirements.

```js
const socket = new WebSocket("wss://websocket.example.org", ["wamp", "soap"]);
socket.addEventListener("open", () => {
  console.log("Negotiated:", socket.protocol);
});
```

## Error event

The `error` event fires when the connection cannot be established, for example when the server is unreachable or the handshake fails. The `error` event is always followed by a `close` event whose `wasClean` is `false`.

The page does not document any properties of the `error` event. The example only logs the event object. The details of a failure are available on the `close` event, which exposes `wasClean`, `code`, and `reason`:

```js
socket.addEventListener("error", (event) => {
  console.error("WebSocket error:", event);
});

socket.addEventListener("close", (event) => {
  if (event.wasClean) {
    console.log(`Closed cleanly, code=${event.code}, reason=${event.reason}`);
  } else {
    console.log("Connection died");
  }
});
```

